" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564444 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d31dd379-95e8-5e67-883a-a09641f1eecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564452 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e84dcffa-6797-50d5-aaea-ce1046d2837d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34925275-ae4c-5d65-95f3-737bbc060369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564481 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564487 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564502 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de9e1bb6-8c29-536b-b329-05255b6d1f04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564529 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
